---
import JournalArticle from '../../components/JournalArticle.astro';
const title='A Diwali greeting card that opens into a 3D scene';
const description='Shubham Shinde built an interactive 3D Diwali greeting card with draggable objects, day and night scenes, fireworks, and a personalisable flip card.';
const poster='assets/journal/diwali/greeting-night.jpg';
---
<JournalArticle slug="happy-diwali-3d-card" title={title} description={description} shareImage={poster} shareAlt="Happy Diwali in an interactive 3D scene filled with diyas, kandils, marigolds and lotuses" projectUrl="/experiments/happy-diwali-3d/" projectLabel="Open the interactive Happy Diwali card" keywords={['AI-assisted design','AI-generated 3D models','interactive Diwali greeting card','creative technology','Three.js project']}>
  <figure class="journal-media"><img src="/assets/journal/diwali/greeting-night.jpg" alt="Night view of the Happy Diwali 3D scene, with giant greeting type, glowing diyas, hanging kandils, marigolds and lotuses" width="1920" height="1080" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>The night scene. Open the live card to drag objects, turn the scene and flip it into a personal greeting.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="journal-copy">
    <p>I wanted to make a Diwali greeting that did something when you reached out and touched it. The result starts as a browser-based 3D scene: giant “Happy Diwali” type, clay diyas, hanging kandils, marigolds and lotuses. You can drag the objects, turn the whole scene, switch between day and night, and tap the night sky for fireworks.</p>
    <p>Then the scene becomes a card. Flip it over, change the names and message, and share your greeting. The custom message and object positions are saved in the visitor’s browser.</p>
    <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>What it is</dt><dd>A personalisable 3D Diwali greeting card that runs in a web browser.</dd></div><div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>Three.js, GLTF models and custom interaction physics.</dd></div><div><dt>AI in the process</dt><dd>AI built the experience from my concept prompt; Tripo CLI generated the 3D models.</dd></div><div><dt>Who made it</dt><dd>Shubham Shinde, design lead and AI-assisted creative technologist in Bengaluru, India.</dd></div></dl>
    <h2>AI-generated models, then a lot of hands-on making</h2>
    <p>I generated the 3D models with the Tripo CLI. The diya started from a reference image; the kandil, marigold and lotus started from text prompts. The raw models were around 40 MB each, so I simplified them to roughly 1.8–3 MB with a custom Python script that kept the UV seams closed and texture edges clean.</p>
    <p>AI built the interactive experience from my concept prompt, and Tripo helped me bring the 3D objects into the scene. I made the creative calls around how the objects, lighting, type, motion and card interaction should work together. The goal was for the result to feel like a personal greeting, not a model viewer.</p>
    <div class="journal-gallery" aria-label="Day and night scenes from the interactive Diwali card">
      <figure><img src="/assets/journal/diwali/greeting-day.jpg" alt="Daytime view of the interactive Diwali greeting with marigolds, lotuses, kandils and large greeting text" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Day mode</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="/experiments/happy-diwali-3d/assets/tripo-models.png" alt="A contact sheet of candidate AI-generated 3D models made with Tripo" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Model exploration with Tripo</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <h2>A card you can actually make yours</h2>
    <p>The scene has a playful front and a personal back. I wanted the flip to feel like a proper greeting-card moment: explore the lights and flowers, then turn it over and write something for a real person.</p>
    <p>Open the <a href="/experiments/happy-diwali-3d/">live Happy Diwali 3D greeting</a>. You can also see the <a href="/journal/dither-tool/">Dither Boil image tool</a> and my <a href="/journal/diwali-isometric-motion/">isometric Diwali motion study</a>.</p>
  </section>
</JournalArticle>
